  before you pick.

  [1mBrowser notifications do not reach you when the app is closed — not without a server.[0m The OS-level toast from the
  Notification API only fires while your page is running. Real "closed tab" delivery needs a service worker [3mplus[0m the Web
  Push protocol [3mplus[0m a server holding subscriptions and VAPID keys. You don't have that, and it's a substantial project
  on its own.

  So today, every option below is "only while the tab is open." They differ in how loudly they interrupt you:

  - [1mIn-app only[0m — a bell icon with a count and a dropdown list. Always works, no permission prompt, no OS integration.
    The honest baseline. Costs you: you have to be looking at the tab.
  - [1mIn-app + OS toast[0m — same, plus [38;5;153mNotification.requestPermission()[39m and a desktop toast. Gets your attention in another
    window, *if the tab is still open somewhere. Costs you: a permission prompt, and a user expectation that it works
    when the app is closed — which it won't.
  - [1mFull web push[0m — the thing people actually picture. Requires the backend you don't have. Not on the table today, but
    the in-app model doesn't block it later.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-app only.[0m Not because the toast is hard — it's maybe twenty lines — but because a notification
  you can't act on is worse than none. A toast that fires only when the tab happens to be open trains you to distrust
  it, and you'll ask "why didn't it tell me?" the first time it silently doesn't. Better to have one channel that
  behaves predictably. The toast is trivial to add later once the notification model exists.
